l language ambiguous. Need decide if proposed reading plausible. Need not use own Stage 1? We have Stage 1 earlier, but Stage 2 asks assess independently against requirement and propositions. Keep Stage 1 unchanged, record newly discovered interpretations only in additional_interpretations. We can add if missing. Need likely all entries have additional_interpretations maybe empty unless new. But could add some.\n\nNeed determine for each R whether reading a/b supported. The proposed readings often split universal vs existential, joint vs separate, immediate vs eventual, etc. Need judge natural-language plausibility. We need be consistent.\n\nLet's define general principles:\n- \"must eventually occur\" can be existential (at some future time) or maybe from now eventually. Joint occurrence? If \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" can mean both occur eventually, not necessarily same step. Reading a \"At least one step has both events\" is less plausible but maybe possible? The phrase \"a pulse and a flash\" could be interpreted as conjunction at same step? \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" often means there is some future time where both have occurred (not necessarily simultaneously). Could simultaneous be plausible? Need supported if plausible. For R001 a: At least one step has both events. Is that plausible? \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" could mean a pulse and a flash (possibly together) eventually occur? Maybe not strongly; but \"and\" can conjoin events occurring at same step? Example \"a pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" likely means each event occurs at least once. Simultaneous joint occurrence is not necessary but could be plausible? Need decide. If natural language says \"A pulse and a flash\" could be a compound event? But propositions separate. I lean a not supported? But annotation asks multiple readings may be supported; judge natural-language plausibility, not preferred. Is simultaneous interpretation plausible? \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" could mean there will be a step where both a pulse and a flash occur? Maybe if pulse and flash are actions at same step? But \"a pulse and a flash\" as two events, not necessarily same. Hmm.\n\nNeed maybe infer from pairs: Many requirements have \"A pulse and a flash must eventually occur\" vs \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring\". Proposed readings a joint, b separate. Need choose if both supported? The instruction says Supported means reading is plausible interpretation; need not be only. So if both plausible, mark Supported. But if one clearly implausible, Not supported.\n\nNeed decide for each. Could be from some dataset where they ask identify plausible readings; likely both readings often plausible? But some are not. Need careful.\n\nLet's examine R002: \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring.\" a: Both events coincide infinitely often. b: Each event occurs infinitely often, without required coincidence. Natural: keep occurring plural likely each occurs infinitely often; coincidence not required. Is joint coincidence plausible? \"Pulses and flashes must keep occurring\" could mean pairs? Maybe less plausible but possible? I'd mark a Uncertain or Not supported? Need choose. If plural \"Pulses and flashes\" generally separate. a requires coincidence infinitely often, too strong, not supported. b supported.\n\nR003: \"Every request must be followed by a pulse and a flash.\" a: Every request has a strictly later step with both events. b: Every request has a strictly later pulse and a strictly later flash, possibly at different steps. Natural: followed by a pulse and a flash likely both later, possibly same or different. a (same step) plausible? Maybe if pulse and flash can occur same step? It says followed by a pulse and a flash, could be joint step. But \"strictly later step with both events\" is plausible if both can coincide. However \"followed by\" usually after request, not necessarily same. Could be supported? b definitely. Need maybe a supported? But if \"pulse and a flash\" as two events after request, simultaneous is possible but not required. Natural-language plausibility: yes, could be interpreted as a combined response on a later step. Mark both Supported? Need not overrestrict. But some a readings are existential vs universal. For \"Every request\" universal, existential a \"At least one qualifying pulse...\" for R037 etc likely not supported. Need pattern.\n\nR004: \"A pulse and a flash must occur before any stop.\" a: A joint occurrence is required before first stop, if a stop occurs. b: Each event required before first stop, possibly different. Natural: \"A pulse and a flash must occur before any stop\" means before any stop, both have occurred, not necessarily joint. a joint before stop could be plausible? Maybe not if events can separate. But \"a pulse and a flash\" could be a compound? I'd mark a Uncertain/Not supported? b supported. Need decide.\n\nR005: \"After each request, a pulse and a flash are required before the next stop.\" a: Starting strictly after each request, both events must coincide before any subsequent stop. b: each event separately before stop. Natural: both required before next stop, not necessarily coincide. a too strong but plausible? Maybe not. b supported.\n\nR006: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active for two consecutive steps.\" a: Both states hold throughout same pair of consecutive steps. b: Each state has a pair of consecutive active steps, possibly different pairs. Natural: \"The signal and the indicator\" compound subject, \"must eventually be active for two consecutive steps\" likely both active simultaneously for two steps. a supported. b? Could mean each eventually active for two consecutive steps (possibly separately) because plural? But wording \"the signal and the indicator must eventually be active\" could be distributive? Maybe less plausible. Need maybe b supported? If \"The signal and the indicator\" could be read as both individually? But \"for two consecutive steps\" could apply to each. Hmm. The requirement likely intended joint? But proposed reading b plausible? Need judge natural-language. I'd mark a Supported, b Uncertain? But final needs enum. Could be Supported if plausible. Let's think: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active for two consecutive steps.\" Natural: both the signal and the indicator are active for two consecutive steps (joint). Separate pairs is possible but less natural. Is it plausible? Maybe yes: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually be active\" can mean each eventually active, and \"for two consecutive steps\" can mean each has two consecutive active steps. I'd mark b Supported? But if multiple readings may be supported. Need not only.\n\nR007: \"The signal and the indicator must eventually become active.\" a: At some step both are inactive and at the next both are active. b: Each independently changes from inactive to active at least once. Natural: become active = transition from inactive to active, eventually. Could be joint transition? a too specific (both inactive at previous step). b plausible. a? If \"the signal and the indicator must eventually become active\" could mean both become active (maybe simultaneously) but not necessarily from inactive at same previous step. a requires a step where both inactive and next both active. Not plausible? Maybe not. b supported.\n\nR008: \"Eventually the signal and the indicator must be active until a release occurs.\" a: common episode starts with both active and maintains both until release. b: each has own active episode lasting until a release, possibly different episodes/releases. Natural: eventually both active until release (joint). a supported. b? Could be distributive? \"The signal and the indicator must be active until a release\" likely same release? Maybe separate less plausible. Could mark b Uncertain/Not supported. But maybe \"each has its own active episode until a release\" is plausible if release per? Hmm.\n\nR009: \"A pulse and a flash must occur with a completion afterward.\" a: joint pulse-and-flash step with later completion. b: each event occurs with some later completion, possibly separately. Natural: \"A pulse and a flash must occur with a completion afterward\" could mean both occur before completion, not necessarily joint. a plausible? Maybe if pulse and flash joint? b supported. Need maybe both.\n\nR010: \"After a start, a pulse and a flash must occur before a finish.\" a: Some start precedes a joint occurrence, which precedes a finish. b: Some start precedes both events, may occur separately before finish. Natural: after a start, both before finish. a joint? plausible? b supported.\n\nR011: \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark must eventually occur.\" a: The two two-step sequences have the same starting step. b: Each two-step sequence occurs, possibly starting at different steps. Natural: \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark\" likely two separate sequences; same starting step would mean pulse and click same step? It says a pulse ... and a click ... must eventually occur. Could be simultaneous pairs? Possible but not likely. b supported. a maybe not supported. But if \"and\" conjoins two patterns that must occur, they could occur at same time? \"A pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark\" could be read as a pulse and click at same step followed by flash and mark at next? But propositions: a pulse occurs, b flash occurs, c click occurs, d mark appears. a: two two-step sequences have same starting step (pulse+click at t, flash+mark at t+1). Is that plausible? Maybe yes if events can coincide. But wording \"a pulse immediately followed by a flash and a click immediately followed by a mark\" could mean both sequences eventually occur, not necessarily same start. a too strong but possible. Need decide.\n\nR012: \"The signal and the indicator must keep being active for two consecutive steps.\" a: infinitely many starting steps for common two-step active episode. b: each has infinitely many two-step active episodes, no alignment. Natural: keep being active for two consecutive steps likely joint episodes? a supported. b maybe plausible? \"The signal and the indicator\" individually keep being active? But \"for two consecutive steps\" maybe joint. Could mark b Uncertain/Not supported. Need consistency with R006.\n\nR013: \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash.\" a: flash exactly one step after each pulse. b: flash at some strictly later step. Natural: followed by can mean eventually later or immediately? Both plausible. Mark both Supported? If \"followed by\" in formal contexts often eventually after, not necessarily next. a is stricter but plausible if immediate? Maybe yes.\n\nR014: \"If there is a pulse now, a flash must follow.\" a: pulse at current step requires flash at next step. b: pulse at current step requires flash at some later step. Both plausible? \"follow\" could be next or later. Mark both Supported.\n\nR015: \"There must be at least one pulse followed by a flash.\" a: some pulse has flash at next step. b: some pulse has flash at strictly later step. Both plausible. Mark both Supported.\n\nR016: \"After every pulse, the indicator must then stay on permanently.\" a: indicator is on at every step beginning at next step after each pulse. b: after each pulse, there is a later step from which indicator always on. Natural: \"then stay on permanently\" likely immediately from pulse or next step. a supported. b? \"then\" could allow delay? Maybe less. But \"stay on permanently\" after each pulse could mean eventually enters permanent on? Not likely. Maybe b Uncertain/Not supported. Need choose.\n\nR017: \"A stop must occur, and before the first stop every pulse must be followed by a flash, even if that flash comes after the stop.\" a: first stop eventually occurs, every earlier pulse has flash at next step. b: first stop eventually occurs, every earlier pulse has flash at some strictly later step. Natural: followed by flash, even if after stop, means later not necessarily next. a too strict but plausible? Maybe not. b supported. a maybe Not supported? But \"followed\" can be immediate? However explicit even if after stop suggests not necessarily immediate. Could be next step after pulse, which may be after stop if pulse just before stop? But if pulse earlier, next step could be before stop. Not enough. I'd mark a Uncertain? Need binary? Use Uncertain if cannot decide. But likely a Not supported? Hmm.\n\nR018: \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash, with a chime at some strictly later step than that flash.\" a: each pulse has flash at next step, and chime later than that flash. b: each pulse has flash at some later step, chime still later. Natural: flash followed by chime; flash after pulse not specified immediate. a plausible? Maybe not due explicit strictly later than flash, but pulse->flash could be immediate? \"followed by\" can be immediate. Could mark both Supported? Need maybe a supported because immediate pulse-flash possible, b supported because eventual.\n\nR019: \"A pulse must never be followed by a flash.\" a: No pulse has a flash at next step. b: No pulse has a flash at any strictly later step. Natural: never followed = no later flash. b supported. a too weak? If no later flash implies no next, but a only next. Is a plausible? \"never be followed by a flash\" could mean no immediate next flash? But \"never\" likely any later. a not supported? Could be if \"followed\" means immediate? But with never, likely global. Mark a Not supported, b Supported.\n\nR020: \"Every pulse must be followed by a flash before any subsequent stop, with no stop at the flash step.\" a: Each pulse has a flash without a stop at the next step. b: Each pulse has a later flash, with no stop from the next step through that flash step. Natural: before any subsequent stop and no stop at flash step. a too strict immediate, but plausible? b supported. Maybe a Not supported? Need decide.\n\nR021: \"Every pair of pulses on consecutive steps must be followed by a flash.\" a: Each consecutive pulse pair has flash at step immediately after its second pulse. b: Each pair has flash at some strictly later step than its second pulse. \"followed by\" could immediate or later. Both plausible? a maybe. Mark both Supported? Need maybe b supported.\n\nR022: \"Eventually, the rule that every pulse is followed by a flash must hold from then on.\" a: There is a step after which every pulse has a flash at next step. b: There is a step after which every pulse has a flash at some strictly later step. Natural: rule every pulse followed by flash likely eventual (later). a could be if rule means immediate? Not specified. Both plausible? Maybe a supported? Hmm.\n\nR023: \"A pulse followed by a flash must occur over and over without end.\" a: Infinitely many pulses have flash at next step. b: Infinitely many pulses have flash at some strictly later step. Natural: pattern pulse followed by flash repeats; immediate not required. a plausible? Maybe. Mark both Supported? Need maybe a supported if pattern adjacent? \"followed by\" can immediate.\n\nR024: \"Once an activation occurs, every pulse from that step onward must be followed by a flash.\" a: Beginning at first activation step, every pulse has flash at next step. b: every pulse has flash at some strictly later step. Both plausible? a maybe. Mark both Supported?\n\nR025: \"Requests require an active response.\" a: Every request has active response at its own step. b: Every request has active response at some strictly later step. Natural: require active response could be same step or later. Both plausible. Mark both Supported.\n\nR026: \"A request at the starting step requires an active response.\" a: starting-step request requires response at starting step. b: requires response strictly later. Both plausible? \"at starting step\" only triggers, response maybe same or later. Mark both Supported.\n\nR027: \"There must be a request with an active response.\" a: Some request coincides with active response. b: Some request has active response strictly later. Both plausible? \"with\" could simultaneous or associated later. Mark both Supported.\n\nR028: \"Requests with an active response must keep occurring.\" a: Infinitely many requests coincide with active response. b: Infinitely many requests have active response strictly later. Both plausible? \"Requests with an active response\" suggests simultaneous? But could mean requests that get active response. Mark both Supported? Need maybe a supported, b supported.\n\nR029: \"Eventually, requests must always have an active response.\" a: From some point onward, each request coincides with active response. b: each request has active response strictly later. Both plausible? \"have\" maybe simultaneous or eventual. Mark both Supported.\n\nR030: \"Requests require an active response until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" a: Before first release, every request coincides with active response; release occurs. b: Before first release, every request has active response strictly later; release occurs. Natural: until release, requirement applies; response could be same/later. Both plausible? But if request just before release, strictly later response may occur after release, violating until? Hmm. a maybe supported. b? If response must be before release? Not specified. Could mark a Supported, b Uncertain? Need decide.\n\nR031: \"Requests require an active response unless and until a release occurs.\" a: Every request before first release, or forever if no release, coincides with active response. b: ... has active response strictly later. Similar.\n\nR032: \"From the first release onward, requests require an active response.\" a: At and after first release, every request coincides with active response. b: strictly later. Both plausible? Maybe a supported, b supported.\n\nR033: \"Every request requires a response that remains active forever.\" a: For each request, response is active from request step forever. b: active forever starting at some strictly later step. Natural: response remains active forever, could start same/later. Both plausible. Mark both Supported.\n\nR034: \"Every request requires the response to stay active until a release, which must occur.\" a: Each request starts a requirement for activity at that step and until release occurs. b: Each request has strictly later step starting requirement for activity then and until release. Natural: response stay active until release; start could be request step or later. Both plausible? Maybe b less due \"requires response to stay active\" after request. Could mark both Supported.\n\nR035: \"A request at the next step requires an active response.\" a: request exactly one step from start requires response at that same step. b: requires response at some step after it. Both plausible? \"at next step\" trigger, response maybe same/later. Mark both Supported.\n\nR036: \"Requests made while the response is permitted require an active response.\" a: Every request made when permission holds coincides with active response. b: strictly later. Both plausible. Mark both Supported.\n\nR037: \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash, possibly at the same step.\" a: At least one qualifying pulse has a flash at that step or some later step. b: Every qualifying pulse has a flash at that step or some later step; no pulse required. Natural: \"A pulse is to be followed\" could be universal? In LTL often \"a pulse is to be followed by flash\" might mean every pulse? Or existential? Ambiguous. Proposed a existential, b universal. Need judge plausible. Could be both? If no pulse required, b vacuous. Natural \"A pulse is to be followed...\" often universal over pulses? But could be at least one? Hmm. Need likely mark a Not supported? Let's inspect pattern: R037-R046 readings a existential, b universal. Requirements: \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash, possibly at the same step.\" Could be a rule: whenever pulse occurs, flash later. It doesn't require a pulse. So b supported. a \"At least one qualifying pulse...\" maybe if there is a pulse? But requirement doesn't assert existence. \"A pulse is to be followed\" could be interpreted as there must be a pulse followed by flash? But \"possibly at same step\" maybe existential? Need think. In formal specification, \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash\" could be G(a -> F b) (universal, no pulse required). Not F(a & F b) existential. So a not supported? But natural-language plausibility: Could someone read it as at least one pulse? Maybe less. Need choose. The instruction says use requirement and proposition definitions to identify plausible meanings. It may be about ambiguous readings between universal and existential. Many such entries likely test whether \"a pulse is to be followed\" means all pulses or at least one. Need decide.\n\nFor R037, \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash\" singular indefinite often means every pulse? In rules, \"A pulse is to be followed by a flash\" = whenever a pulse occurs, a flash follows. So b supported, a not supported. But if \"must\" absent? \"is to be\" normative. I'd mark a Not supported, b Supported.\n\nR038: \"A pulse requires the light to be on at that step.\" Universal rule: every pulse requires light at same step. a existential not supported, b supported.\n\nR039: \"A pulse is to have a flash at the next step.\" universal immediate. a existential not supported, b supported.\n\nR040: \"A pulse requires the light to stay on from that step onward.\" universal. a not supported, b supported.\n\nR041: \"A pulse calls for the light to be on until a release occurs, with a release required.\" universal? a existential not supported, b supported? But release required maybe for each pulse? Need b says every qualifying pulse has a release at or after its step and light on from pulse up to excluding release; no pulse required. Plausible. a existential not.\n\nR042: \"A pulse calls for the light to stay on until a release, or forever if no release comes.\" universal. a not, b supported.\n\nR043: \"A pulse calls for the light to be on at the first release at or after that pulse, and that release must occur.\" universal. a not, b supported.\n\nR044: \"A pulse calls for a release before any flash from that step onward, and a flash at or after the release.\" universal. a not, b supported.\n\nR045: \"A pulse while enabled calls for a flash at that step or later.\" universal over enabled pulses. a not, b supported.\n\nR046: \"Before the first stop, a pulse calls for a flash at that step or later.\" universal over pulses before first stop. a not, b supported.\n\nBut need consider if existential could be plausible for some? The phrase \"A pulse\" can be generic universal. So likely a Not supported, b Supported for R037-R046.\n\nR047: \"The first and second indicators must not be active now.\" a: At current step, at least one indicator is inactive. b: At current step, each indicator is inactive. Natural: \"the first and second indicators must not be active\" means both not active. b supported. a weaker (at least one) not supported? Could \"must not be active\" distributive? \"The first and second indicators must not be active\" = neither active. a not.\n\nR048: \"The first and second indicators must never be active.\" a: At every step, at least one indicator is inactive. b: At every step, each indicator inactive. Natural: both never active = neither ever active. b supported. a not (could have one active if other inactive). Not supported.\n\nR049: \"At some point, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" a: Some step has at least one inactive indicator. b: Some step has each indicator inactive. Natural: both not active at some point. b supported. a not.\n\nR050: \"Infinitely often, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" a: Infinitely many steps have at least one inactive indicator. b: Infinitely many steps have each indicator inactive. Natural: both inactive infinitely often. b supported. a not.\n\nR051: \"From some point onward, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" a: Eventually every remaining step has at least one inactive indicator, with inactive indicator allowed to vary. b: Eventually each indicator stays inactive forever. Natural: both inactive from some point onward. b supported. a not.\n\nR052: \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs, and a release must occur.\" a: release eventually; at each earlier step at least one inactive. b: at each earlier step each inactive. Natural: both inactive until release. b supported. a not.\n\nR053: \"The first and second indicators must not be active until a release occurs; release is optional.\" a: before first release, at least one inactive; if no release forever. b: before first release, each inactive; if no release forever. b supported. a not.\n\nR054: \"Whenever the gate is open, the first and second indicators must not be active.\" a: Every open-gate step has at least one inactive indicator. b: Every open-gate step has each indicator inactive. Natural: both not active when gate open. b supported. a not.\n\nR055: \"Whenever a pulse occurs, the first and second indicators must not be active at the immediately following step.\" a: step immediately after every pulse has at least one inactive. b: each inactive. Natural: both inactive. b supported. a not.\n\nR056: \"Whenever a pulse occurs, there must be a step at that time or later when the first and second indicators are not active.\" a: current or later step with at least one inactive. b: current or later step with each inactive. Natural: \"the first and second indicators are not active\" = both inactive. b supported. a not? C